===Etymology 1===
Shortened from {{m|en|gooney}}, from obsolete ''gony'' ("simpleton", circa 1580), of {{unk|en|title=unknown}} origin. Perhaps a familiar term derived from {{der|en|enm|gone}}, a variant of {{m|enm|gome|t=man, person}}. ''Gony'' was applied by sailors to the [[albatross]] and similar big, clumsy birds (circa 1839). '''Goon''' first carried the meaning "stupid person" (circa 1921).
* The meaning of "hired thug" (circa 1938) is largely influenced by the comic strip character [[w:Alice the Goon|Alice the Goon]] from the ''[[w:Popeye|Popeye]]'' series.
* The "fool" sense was reinforced by the popular radio program, ''[[w:The Goon Show|The Goon Show]]'', starring [[w:Spike Milligan|Spike Milligan]] and [[w:Peter Sellers|Peter Sellers]].
* The "guard" sense was influenced by both senses 1 and 2, though not by the Goon Show reference, which arose about 10 years after WWII.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[thug]]; a usually muscular [[henchman]] with little intelligence.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 22, 2009|author=Kevin Baker|title=Blood on the Street|work=New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2009/02/22/books/review/Baker-t.html
|passage=Efforts to unionize were routinely met with clubbings, shootings, jailings, blacklistings and executions, perpetrated not only by well-armed legions of company '''goons''', but also by police officers, deputies, National Guardsmen and even regular soldiers.}}
# A [[fool]]; someone who is [[silly]], [[stupid]], [[awkward]], or [[outlandish]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=5|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Mr. Campion appeared suitably impressed and she warmed to him. He was very easy to talk to with those long clown lines in his pale face, a natural '''goon''', born rather too early she suspected.}}
# {{lb|en|ice hockey|pejorative}}&nbsp; An {{l|en|enforcer|id=hockey}} or [[fighter]].
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2002|title=Hit Somebody!|artist={{w|Warren Zevon}}|passage={{...}}a scout from the Flames came down from Saskatoon, said, "There's always room on our team for a '''goon'''"}}
# {{lb|en|UK|WWII|PoW slang}} A German guard in a prisoner-of-war camp.
# {{lb|en|slang}} One hired to legally kidnap a child and forcibly transport them to a boot camp, boarding school, wilderness therapy, or a similar rehabilitation facility.
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/23/theater/therapy-becomes-theater-in-wilderness.html|work=New York Times|passage=Owen Jenney, Ms. Hamburger's son, got gooned, though he said his '''goons''' turned out to be "really nice guys, actually." He is 19 now, a freshman in college, and he remembers arriving in the wilderness frightened and confused, angrily convinced that sending him across the country to Oregon was "way out of proportion" to the situation.|title=Therapy Becomes Theater in 'Wilderness'|date=2016-10-20|author=Laura Collins-Hughes|brackets=1}}
# {{lb|en|internet slang}} A member of the [[comedy]] [[web site]] {{w|Something Awful}}.

